I would suggest learning some more basic R visualization methods for the things you are trying to do, as there are a lot of things that are possible that Cardinal doesn't provide out-of-the-box, but are very easy to do once you're more familiar with some other standard R packages.

In particular, the ggplot2 visualization package would be quite helpful to you.

Hadley Wickam's R for Data Science is a good intro that covers visualization with ggplot2: https://r4ds.had.co.nz

As far as Cardinal goes, you just need to get familiar with extracting summaries (usually mean spectra, I'm assuming) from datasets and their ROIs. In Cardinal 2.6, the summarizeFeatures(obj, FUN="mean", as="DataFrame") method will do this for you. You can then coerce the resulting MassDataFrame into an ordinary data.frame for input to ggplot2 to create any plots you want.

-Kylie

